WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.850
There are, it seems, no depths of Star Wars
obscurity which John Favreau will not plumb,

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:08.650
no peak of nerd minutiae that he will not
summit.

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:13.200
From the practical purpose of an ice cream
maker in space to the return of "maclunkey,"

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.930
The Mandalorian has now spent two full seasons
sinking its teeth into some of the most pedantic

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:21.840
references available, met at every turn with
fan appreciation.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.700
In the closing moments of the show's second
season finale, longtime fans got a big surprise

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:31.550
in the form of a return visit to the Star
Wars galaxy's most evocatively smelly-looking

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:36.250
den of scum and villainy: Jabba's Palace,
last seen in live action during the events

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:37.550
of Return of the Jedi.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:41.780
For an all-too-brief moment, viewers got to
catch up with old friends like Background

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:47.720
Weequay, Enslaved Twi'lek Number 14, and,
best of all, Bib Fortuna, Jabba's sniveling

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:49.330
toady from Episode VI.

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:51.440
And the showrunners didn't stop there.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:56.040
True to form, they included an even more obscure
reference in their post-credits sting for

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.510
The Book of Boba Fett.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:01.100
Unlike some of their other callbacks, you
didn't even need to be an obsessive fan to

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:06.250
spot it — you just needed to have $2.99
back in 1983.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:10.650
It all goes back to the original trilogy,
merchandising, and the fact that action figures,

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.860
which absolutely are not dolls, need to accessorize.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:18.280
Star Wars action figures were especially well
regarded in the field of adding weird extra

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:22.970
bits of plastic to sweeten the deal for kids,
with each addition nearly tripling the odds

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:26.260
of losing part of your favorite toy to a vacuum
cleaner.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:31.260
An especially popular accoutrement for any
given action figure was a vaguely stick-shaped

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.260
thing.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:34.690
Admiral Ackbar had a stick thing for some
reason.

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:36.320
So did General Madine.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:37.320
And Bib Fortuna?

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.610
Well, Bib Fortuna had the most stick-shaped
thing of all.

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:45.409
Yes, Kenner's original Return of the Jedi
tie-in Bib Fortuna action figure carried with

00:01:45.409 --> 00:01:50.360
him a long, twisty staff — a weird detail,
considering that the character never had one

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:51.850
in the movie, but hey.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:56.950
"Gross pale fleshy-headed criminal guy" is
a tough sell, and kids love walking sticks,

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:57.950
right?

00:01:57.950 --> 00:01:58.950
Maybe.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:02.280
This was the same staff that Favreau and company
gave to Bib in his brief appearance at the

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.950
end of The Mandalorian's second season finale.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:09.129
With any luck, this won't be the last action
figure callback in the series, and next season

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:13.480
will feature the return of Luke Skywalker,
now with Collector Coin and Saber Slam Action

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:14.480
Attack.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:15.989
Batteries sold separately, of course.

00:02:15.989 --> 00:02:20.319
However, by this point, you're probably wondering
who this Bib Fortuna guy is, in the first

00:02:20.319 --> 00:02:21.319
place.

00:02:21.319 --> 00:02:25.120
Sure, he was in Jabba's palace, and you know
he's the guy with the bumpy tentacle head,

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.890
but every Star Wars character has a super-elaborate
backstory, don't they?

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:33.299
And maybe, somewhere in that backstory, there
might be an explanation for his staff?

00:02:33.299 --> 00:02:39.290
Thankfully, the mighty Star Wars-loving aficionados
at Wookieepedia have foreseen this great question,

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:44.180
and are here to answer any and all questions
about who Bib Fortuna is, on a deeper level.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:48.069
Unfortunately, it seems that the history of
his staff — in canon, that is — remains

00:02:48.069 --> 00:02:53.139
unanswered, but thankfully, some details about
Bib Fortuna himself have been established.

00:02:53.139 --> 00:02:58.040
He is a Twi'lek from the planet Ryloth, who
at some point rose to become the second-in-command

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.610
to Jabba the Hutt, Tatooine's infamous crime
lord.

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:04.530
As it happens, the rancor seen in Return of
the Jedi — you know, the one they try to

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:09.709
kill off Luke with — was previously a birthday
present that Bib Fortuna gave to his master,

00:03:09.709 --> 00:03:13.129
which is, frankly, about as weird as birthday
presents get.

00:03:13.129 --> 00:03:16.889
Earning this prestigious position was, for
the record, a tough battle.

00:03:16.889 --> 00:03:21.671
His main competitor for the role, a guy named
Bidlo Kwerve, ended up being sacrificed to

00:03:21.671 --> 00:03:26.450
the aforementioned rancor, and has thus only
been seen on film as a usefully-placed skull

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:27.799
in Return of the Jedi.

00:03:27.799 --> 00:03:33.230
Dubious hiring practices aside, Bib Fortuna
worked alongside his master for decades, going

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:35.609
back all the way to at least The Phantom Menace.

00:03:35.609 --> 00:03:39.999
Following Jabba's death, The Mandalorian shows
that he took great pleasure in gaining control

00:03:39.999 --> 00:03:45.430
of his former boss' palace, slaves, and fortunes,
up until his rather unfortunate (for him)

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:46.939
encounter with Boba Fett.

00:03:46.939 --> 00:03:50.359
Behind the scenes, Bib Fortuna has been played
by a few actors.

00:03:50.359 --> 00:03:54.769
Scottish actor Michael Carter was the original,
and the character's subsequent appearances

00:03:54.769 --> 00:03:59.269
in both The Phantom Menace and The Mandalorian
have seen Matthew Wood take the role.

00:03:59.269 --> 00:04:00.540
Is he coming back?

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:01.540
Probably not.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:05.730
As you saw in The Mandalorian, it seems that
Bib Fortuna has met his maker, and ascended

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:09.989
into whatever mysterious afterlife the Star
Wars universe possesses.

00:04:09.989 --> 00:04:14.150
But hey, at least he got to finally hold that
cool action figure-style staff before kicking

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:15.590
the bucket, right?

